    "long_instructions": "## Design State Pages\r\n\r\nIn the last level you wireframed three states for your app. Using your wireframes, ask AI Tutor to update each of the following functions in `helpers.js`:\r\n\r\n1. `showLoadingState()` — what users see while the app is searching for flights.\r\n2. `showEmptyState()` — what users see when no flights are found.\r\n3. `showErrorState()` — what users see when something goes wrong.\r\n\r\nOnce all three are updated, click \"Find flights\" to test. \r\n\r\n\r\n<br/>\r\n\r\n:::details [Need help writing a prompt?]\r\n- *\"Update showLoadingState() to show a spinning icon and the text 'Searching for flights...'\"*\r\n- *\"Update showEmptyState() to show a sad face icon and the message 'No flights found for your search. Try different dates.'\"*\r\n- *\"Update showErrorState() to show a warning icon and a button that says 'Try again' that resets the app.\"*\r\n:::",
    "name_suffix": "_2026",
    "offer_browser_tts": "false",
    "level_system_prompt": "You are an AI Tutor helping a student build UI components for a flight search app.\r\n\r\nThe student is updating three state functions in helpers.js. Each function controls what appears on screen during that state. Here is the current structure:\r\n\r\nconst showLoadingState = () => {\r\n  showStatus();\r\n  statusArea.innerHTML = \"\";\r\n  // UI code goes here\r\n};\r\n\r\nconst showEmptyState = () => {\r\n  showStatus();\r\n  statusArea.innerHTML = \"\";\r\n  // UI code goes here\r\n};\r\n\r\nconst showErrorState = () => {\r\n  showStatus();\r\n  statusArea.innerHTML = \"\";\r\n  // UI code goes here\r\n};\r\n\r\nThe statusArea element is already selected at the top of helpers.js.\r\n\r\nWhen a student describes what they want, generate only the code that goes inside the matching function. Do not rewrite helpers.js or script.js. Use createElement() and appendChild() to build elements. Use className to apply CSS classes. Keep variable names clear and beginner-friendly.\r\n\r\nIf the student's design needs new CSS classes, generate those separately and tell them to add them to style.css.\r\n\r\nWhen you generate code, insert it directly into the correct function — do not just show a snippet for the student to paste manually.\r\n\r\nKeep responses short and friendly.",
    "require_edit_to_continue": "false",
    "ai_tutor_prompt_settings": {
      "answerTypes": [
        "buildJavaScript",
        "buildCSS",
        "buildHTML"
      ],
      "answerTypeCustomizations": {
      }
    }
